  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Space Exploration Technologies obligated $2.3B in federal contracts during FY2026 to date. Space Exploration Technologies won 73 awards so far, with an average award size of $31M. Space Exploration Technologies saw obligations down 24.5% year over year, after peaking at $3.4B in FY24 and falling from $3B in FY25 to $2.3B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $2.1B to Space Exploration Technologies in the current fiscal year. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration is the second buyer, obligating $156M. The largest markets for Space Exploration Technologies include Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $1.6B and Satellite Telecommunications at $417M; Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences follows at $250M. Other market categories show much smaller totals, and some line items record zero or nominal values. These figures are fiscal year to date and reflect contract obligations, except where award-value tables specify total contract value.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Space Exploration Technologies' federal business trended recently?",
        "body": "Space Exploration Technologies' obligations rose from $1.5B in FY21 to $3.4B in FY24, then declined to $3B in FY25 and $2.3B in FY26. Space Exploration Technologies' obligations are down 24.5% year over year, reflecting a pullback from the FY24 peak. Space Exploration Technologies continues to receive large single-agency support, with the Department of Defense obligating $2.1B in FY2026 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest federal customers and what do they buy from Space Exploration Technologies?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer, obligating $2.1B, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $156M. Space Exploration Technologies' largest market is Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $1.6B, with Satellite Telecommunications at $417M and R&D in the physical and engineering sciences at $250M. Other identified NAICS markets show minimal or zero obligations."
      }
    ]
